## Preliminaries: start the provers

The shared adapter loads the vendored engines, applies their upstream
preprocessing, and records calls to the real recursive search predicates.
It supplies no answers or task definitions. Choose `:port` or `:nif` here;
the lesson uses the same API with either backend.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
project = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
Code.require_file(Path.join(project, "examples/showcase.exs"))
backend = :port
{:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)
:ok = SWI.load(session, Path.join(project, "examples/prover_showcase.pl"))
```

Keep this session through the lesson and close it in the final cell. Before
replacing it, run that close cell or restart the notebook runtime.
The diagnostic wraps shared SWI predicates, so run these examples serially
with other prover operations in the same embedded runtime.

## Follow a quantified function chain

### What you will learn

Separate the formula you ask about from the prover that searches for its proof.
Our assumptions are `p(a)` and `∀x(p(x) → p(f(x)))`. Repeatedly applying the
second assumption should establish `p(f(f(f(f(a)))))`.

| Syntax              | Meaning                                  |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `A,B`               | conjunction                              |
| `A;B`               | disjunction                              |
| `A=>B`              | implication                              |
| `~A`                | negation                                 |
| `all X:A`, `ex X:A` | universal and existential quantification |

Generate this family of formulas in Elixir; the proof search stays in Prolog.
The four-step instance adapts Otten's tutorial chain family.

```elixir
chain = fn steps ->
  term = Enum.reduce(1..steps, "a", fn _, term -> "f(#{term})" end)
  "(p(a),(all X:(p(X)=>p(f(X)))))=>p(#{term})"
end

four_steps = chain.(4)
Kino.Text.new(four_steps)
```

Run the same formula through leanHaT in Here-and-There (HT) logic and through
nanoCoP in HT and intuitionistic logic. Every run checks its expected verdict
and records positive kernel call and inference counts.

```elixir
run = fn name, prover, logic, formula, expected ->
  answer = ProverShowcase.run(session, prover, logic, formula)
  assert answer["Result"] == {:atom, expected}
  if prover == :nanocop and expected == "proved", do: assert(answer["Proof"] != [])

  %{
    problem: name,
    prover: prover,
    logic: logic,
    result: expected,
    kernel_calls: answer["Calls"],
    inferences: answer["Inferences"]
  }
end

chain_results =
  for {prover, logic} <- [leanhat: :ht, nanocop: :ht, nanocop: :intu] do
    run.("Function chain 4", prover, logic, four_steps, "proved")
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(chain_results)
```

The 20-step version is the longer tutorial instance. We run it in nanoCoP's
intuitionistic mode; HT search can be much more expensive on this family.

```elixir
long_chain = run.("Function chain 20", :nanocop, :intu, chain.(20), "proved")
Kino.DataTable.new([long_chain])
```

### Try it yourself

Change four steps to six. Predict whether the verdict or only the search work
should change. Update the displayed label when changing the formula.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The universal implication can be instantiated again at every step. The formula
still has a constructive proof. Calls and elapsed work can change substantially;
a timeout would mean the run is inconclusive.

</details>

## Prove a finite pigeonhole principle

Four pigeons each occupy one of three holes. Some pair must share a hole.
`o12` means pigeon 1 occupies hole 2. The antecedent states the alternatives
for every pigeon; the consequent lists all possible pair collisions.

This is the complete generator for the tutorial's four-by-three instance.
There are four placement assumptions and eighteen candidate collisions.

```elixir
pigeonhole = fn holes ->
  placements =
    for pigeon <- 1..(holes + 1) do
      "(" <> Enum.map_join(1..holes, ";", &"o#{pigeon}#{&1}") <> ")"
    end

  collisions =
    for hole <- 1..holes,
        first <- 1..(holes + 1),
        second <- 1..(holes + 1),
        first < second,
        do: "(o#{first}#{hole},o#{second}#{hole})"

  "(" <> Enum.join(placements, ",") <> ")=>(" <> Enum.join(collisions, ";") <> ")"
end

pigeon_formula = pigeonhole.(3)
Kino.Text.new(pigeon_formula)
```

```elixir
pigeon_results =
  for logic <- [:ht, :intu] do
    run.("Four pigeons, three holes", :nanocop, logic, pigeon_formula, "proved")
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(pigeon_results)
```

## Compare two logics

HT and intuitionistic logic do not prove exactly the same formulas.
These contrasting cases follow the [Otten/Schaub HT paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2601.03848v1);
double-negation elimination is an additional standard negative control.
Predict the outcomes before evaluating the editable case list.

```elixir
logic_cases = [
  %{name: "Prelinearity", formula: "(p=>q);(q=>p)", ht: "proved", intu: "exhausted"},
  %{
    name: "Quantified witness",
    formula: "ex Y:all X:(p(Y)=>p(X))",
    ht: "proved",
    intu: "exhausted"
  },
  %{name: "Excluded middle", formula: "p; ~p", ht: "exhausted", intu: "exhausted"},
  %{
    name: "Double-negation elimination",
    formula: "(~ ~p)=>p",
    ht: "exhausted",
    intu: "exhausted"
  }
]

logic_results =
  for example <- logic_cases,
      {prover, logic} <- [leanhat: :ht, nanocop: :ht, nanocop: :intu] do
    run.(example.name, prover, logic, example.formula, Map.fetch!(example, logic))
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(logic_results)
```

`proved` means the invoked prover found a proof. `exhausted` means that its
search returned without one. The negative examples above have independent
countermodels; failure on an arbitrary new formula is not a general invalidity
test. nanoCoP's HT embedding is incomplete. A timeout raises `SWI.Error` and
leaves the logical verdict **unknown**; none is accepted as a passing result.

## Inspect a returned proof

nanoCoP can return its actual connection proof term through the wrapper.
Here we display an intuitionistic chain proof, so it concerns the formula
shown above directly. HT mode proves an intuitionistic translation instead.
The notebook displays the term without independently checking the certificate.

```elixir
proof = ProverShowcase.run(session, :nanocop, :intu, four_steps)
assert proof["Proof"] != []
Kino.Text.new(inspect(proof["Proof"], pretty: true, limit: :infinity))
```

leanHaT exposes success here; its search-bound argument is not a proof object.
The following table asks SWI where the real recursive kernels were loaded.

```elixir
kernels =
  for {prover, expected} <- [leanhat: "leanhat_swi.pl", nanocop: "nanocopi20_swi.pl"] do
    [row] =
      SWI.query(session, "prover_showcase:source(#{prover},File,Line)") |> Enum.to_list()

    {:atom, file} = row["File"]
    assert Path.basename(file) == expected
    %{prover: prover, loaded_kernel: Path.basename(file)}
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(kernels)
```

Kernel counts demonstrate execution. They include failed branches; inference
counts also include parsing, preprocessing, and diagnostics. They are not an
independent soundness check or a performance comparison without instrumentation.

